ories and approaches in social research.</article-title><source>American Psychologist</source><year>1981</year><volume>36</volume><fpage>184</fpage><lpage>191</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Cassidy</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Self-categorization, coping and psychological health among unemployed mid-career executives.</article-title><source>Counselling Psychology Quarterly</source><year>2001</year><volume>14</volume><fpage>303</fpage><lpage>315</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Aronsson</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Gustafsson</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Dallner</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Work environment and health in different types of temporary jobs.</article-title><source>European Journal of Work and Organizational Psychology</source><year>2002</year><volume>11</volume><fpage>151</fpage><lpage>175</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Virtanen</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Vahtera</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Kivim&#x000e4;ki</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Pentti</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Ferrie</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Employment security and health.</article-title><source>J Epidemiol Community Health</source><year>2002</year><volume>56</volume><fpage>569</fpage><lpage>574</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12118045</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Aronsson</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>G&#x000f6;ransson</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Permanent employment but not in a preferred occupation: Psychological and medical aspects, research implications.</article-title><source>Journal of Occupational Health Psychology</source><year>1999</year><volume>4</volume><fpage>152</fpage><lpage>163</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10212867</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>McKee-Ryan</surname><given-names>FM</given-names></name><name><surname>Kinicki</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Coping with job loss: A life-facet model.</article-title><source>International Review of Industrial and Organizational Psychology</source><year>2002</year><volume>17</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>29</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zapf</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Dormann</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Frese</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Longitudinal studies in organizational stress research: A review of the literature with reference to methodological issues.</article-title><source>J Occup Health Psychol</source><year>1996</year><volume>1</volume><fpage>145</fpage><lpage>169</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9547043</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Karasek</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name><name><surname>Theorell</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Healthy work. Stress, productivity, and the reconstruction of working life.</article-title><source>Basic Books</source><year>1990</year><publisher-name>New York </publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Artazcoz</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Artieda</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Borrell</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Cortes</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Benach</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Garcia</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Combining job and family demands and being healthy: what are the differences between men and women?</article-title><source>European Journal of Public Health</source><year>2004</year><volume>14</volume><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15080390</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Malmberg-Heimonen</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Julkunen</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Equal opportunities, true options or hidden unemployment? A comparative experience on labour-market marginality.</article-title><source>Int J Soc Welfare</source><year>2002</year><volume>11</volume><fpage>120</fpage><lpage>131</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B25"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Warr</surname><given-names>PB</given-names></name><name><surname>Cook</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Wall</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Scales for the measurement of some work attitudes and aspects of psychological well-being</article-title><source>J Occup Psych</source><year>1979</year><volume>52</volume><fpage>129</fpage><lpage>148</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B26"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Isaksson</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><s